# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH1 sources and time
c = p.chapter("sources", "History, sources and time")
L = LS(c, "sources-time", "What is history? Sources and the measurement of time",
    ["Define history and explain why we study it", "Distinguish primary and secondary sources and give examples", "Explain oral tradition, archaeology and written records", "Use centuries, decades and BC/AD to place dates on a timeline"],
    [("text", "**History** is the study of the past: what people did, why, and with what results. We study it to understand our identity, learn from past mistakes and successes, and understand the world today. Historians find information in **sources**. A **primary source** comes from the time being studied (a letter, a photograph, a tool, a treaty, the memory of an eye-witness). A **secondary source** is written later, using primary sources (a textbook, an encyclopaedia)."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Kinds of source", "- **Oral tradition**: stories, songs, proverbs and genealogies passed by word of mouth by elders, storytellers and royal singers.\n- **Archaeology**: digging up and studying objects, bones, tools, pots and buildings.\n- **Written sources**: books, letters, records, newspapers.\n- Others: photographs, films, recordings, languages and customs."),
     ("key", "methode", "Measuring time", "Dates are counted from the traditional birth of Christ: years before it are **BC** (counted backwards: 500 BC is earlier than 100 BC) and years after are **AD**. A **decade** is 10 years, a **century** is 100 years and a **millennium** is 1000 years. The **19th century** is the years 1801–1900; to find the century of a year, add one to the hundreds: 1884 is in the 19th century."),
     ("text", "A **timeline** is a line on which events are placed in order. **Chronology** means arranging events in the order in which they happened. Historians check sources carefully: Who wrote it? When? Why? Is the author biased? Is the evidence confirmed by other sources? Oral tradition may change as it is retold, and written sources can be one-sided, so we compare several sources."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- A textbook is a secondary source, even if it is old.\n- Years BC run backwards: 300 BC is earlier than 200 BC.\n- The 19th century is 1801–1900, not the 1900s. The year 1900 itself belongs to the 19th century.")],
    [("Example 1: century", "In which century did the Germano-Duala treaty of 1884 take place, and in which century did Cameroon's independence in 1960 take place?", ["For 1884, the hundreds figure is 18, so add one: 19th century.", "For 1960, the hundreds figure is 19, so add one: 20th century.", "So the 19th and the 20th."], "1884 is in the 19th century; 1960 is in the 20th century."),
     ("Example 2: primary or secondary?", "A village elder tells you what he saw at a market 60 years ago. A history book describes the same market using interviews. Which is primary and which is secondary?", ["The elder was an eye-witness.", "The book was written later using other sources.", "So the elder's account is primary and the book is secondary."], "The elder: primary; the book: secondary.")],
    F(tlfig(1880, 1965, [(1884, "1884"), (1914, "1914"), (1945, "1945"), (1960, "1960")], [(1884, 1916, "German rule", "orange")], h=200), "A timeline", "A timeline from 1880 to 1965 with four dates and a period marked German rule."),
    [MCQ("Which of these is a primary source for the history of your school?", "an old photograph of the school opening", ["a modern textbook about education", "a school website written last week", "a quiz on the internet"], "A primary source comes from the time studied."),
     TF("The 19th century covers the years 1801 to 1900.", True, "A century starts with the year ending in 01."),
     MATCH("Match each source to its type.", [("Elders' story passed through generations", "Oral tradition"), ("Pot dug from the ground", "Archaeology"), ("Letter written in 1950", "Written source")], "Different kinds of evidence."),
     NUM("A boy is told an event happened 3 decades ago. How many years ago was it?", 30, "3 × 10 = 30.", unit="years", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain two weaknesses of oral tradition as a historical source and how historians reduce them.", "Stories may change over time or be shaped by the teller's interests, and details such as dates may be lost. Historians compare several oral accounts and check them against archaeology and written records.", "1 mark per weakness; 1 mark for checking against other sources.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A class studies the history of its town.",
          [("mcq", "An interview with an old resident is", "oral evidence", ["a map", "a legend", "a fossil"], "It is spoken evidence.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The year 1760 AD is in the", "18th century", ["17th century", "19th century", "16th century"], "1760: 17 + 1 = 18.", {}),
           ("tf", "Chronology means putting events in the order in which they happened.", True, "That is the meaning of the term.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two reasons for studying history.", "To understand who we are and where we come from, to learn from the successes and mistakes of the past, to understand the present and to develop critical thinking.", "1 mark per reason.", {})])],
    [("A source created at the time of the event is a", "primary source", ["secondary source", "legend only", "modern copy"], "Primary sources come from the time studied."),
     ("A period of 100 years is a", "century", ["decade", "millennium", "generation of 20 years"], "Century means 100 years."),
     ("The abbreviation AD is used for years", "after the traditional birth of Christ", ["before it", "of kings only", "with no date"], "AD counts forwards."),
     ("Digging up and studying old objects is called", "archaeology", ["geography", "economics", "civics"], "Archaeologists use physical evidence."),
     ("A timeline is used to", "show events in order", ["measure rain", "draw maps", "count crops"], "Chronology is displayed on a timeline.")],
    ["Teacher to verify definitions of source types and the convention BC/AD (or BCE/CE) in the syllabus; the dates on the timeline are well-known events used only as examples."], tag="Form 1 history sources")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH2 early humans
c = p.chapter("early", "Early humans and the beginning of farming")
L = LS(c, "early-humans", "Early humans and the Stone Age",
    ["Describe how the earliest humans lived", "Explain the importance of tools and fire", "Distinguish the Old and New Stone Age", "Explain why Africa is important for the story of humankind"],
    [("text", "The earliest humans lived a very long time ago, many hundreds of thousands of years before writing. Fossils and tools found by archaeologists show that humans began to develop in **Africa**, especially in East and Southern Africa, and later spread to other continents. For this reason Africa is often called the **cradle of humankind**."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Old Stone Age", "In the **Old Stone Age** (Palaeolithic) people were **hunter-gatherers**: they hunted animals, fished and gathered fruits, roots and honey. They moved from place to place following food, lived in caves, rock shelters or simple huts, and made tools from **stone**, **bone** and **wood**. They learned to **control fire**."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Why tools and fire mattered", "Tools helped people to cut, scrape, hunt and defend themselves. **Fire** gave warmth, light and protection from wild animals, cooked food and hardened wooden tools. People also developed **language**, worked in groups and made **paintings** on rock walls in many parts of the world, including Africa."),
     ("text", "Later, in the **New Stone Age** (Neolithic), people made finer polished stone tools, made pottery, and — most importantly — began to **farm**. Groups no longer had to roam all the time. We use the names 'Old' and 'New' for stages of development, not for fixed dates, because they began at different times in different places."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Early humans did not all live in caves: many used open camps and huts.\n- 'Stone Age' does not mean people only used stone; they also used wood and bone, which rot and rarely survive.\n- Our evidence is incomplete: new discoveries may change what historians say.")],
    [("Example 1: how did they live?", "Describe how a group of Old Stone Age people got their food.", ["They did not grow crops.", "They hunted and fished and collected wild fruits, roots and honey.", "They moved when food became scarce."], "By hunting, fishing and gathering wild foods, moving as needed."),
     ("Example 2: uses of fire", "Give two ways in which the use of fire helped early people.", ["Fire gave warmth and light.", "It protected them from animals and allowed cooking.", "Cooked food is easier to eat and safer."], "Warmth, protection, cooking.")],
    F(flow("Stone Age steps", ["Hunting and|gathering", "Tools and|fire", "Pottery and|polished tools", "Farming|begins"], per_row=4), "Steps in the Stone Age", "Four boxes from hunting and gathering to the beginning of farming."),
    [MCQ("Early humans who hunted and collected wild food were", "hunter-gatherers", ["farmers", "traders", "kings"], "They did not grow crops."),
     TF("The earliest humans developed in Africa.", True, "Most of the evidence comes from Africa."),
     MATCH("Match each term with its meaning.", [("Fossil", "Remains of an ancient living thing in rock"), ("Hunter-gatherer", "Person who lives by hunting and collecting wild food"), ("Cave painting", "Picture made on a cave wall")], "Early history vocabulary."),
     MCQ("What was the most important change in the New Stone Age?", "the beginning of farming", ["the use of television", "the use of cars", "the invention of printing"], "Farming changed how people lived.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain why fire was one of the most useful discoveries of early people.", "Fire gave warmth and light, kept wild animals away, allowed food to be cooked and made it safe, and hardened wooden tools. It also allowed people to gather and talk in the evening.", "1 mark each for three correct uses.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A museum shows stone tools, bones and a painted rock.",
          [("mcq", "These objects are evidence from", "archaeology", ["geography", "a modern newspaper", "a law book"], "They come from the ground.", {}),
           ("tf", "The Old Stone Age people lived mainly by farming.", False, "They were hunter-gatherers.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Which tool material rots away easily and rarely survives?", "wood", ["stone", "bone", "flint only"], "Stone survives best.", {}),
           ("open", "State two ways early people used rock walls or caves.", "As shelter and for painting pictures of animals and people.", "1 mark per way.", {})])],
    [("The Old Stone Age is also called the", "Palaeolithic", ["Neolithic", "Iron Age", "Bronze Age"], "Palaeo means old."),
     ("Which was a feature of the Old Stone Age?", "moving from place to place for food", ["building big cities", "using computers", "having a king"], "Hunter-gatherers moved."),
     ("Africa is called the cradle of humankind because", "early human fossils are found there", ["it is the largest continent", "it is the hottest", "it has most people"], "Fossil discoveries are important."),
     ("Fire helped early people to", "cook and keep safe", ["print books", "build cars", "write letters"], "Fire had many uses."),
     ("The New Stone Age is also called the", "Neolithic", ["Palaeolithic", "Medieval period", "Atomic age"], "Neo means new.")],
    ["Teacher to verify the terms and approximate time ranges; this lesson gives no numerical dates for human origins on purpose."], tag="Form 1 early humans")

L = LS(c, "farming-iron", "The farming revolution and the use of iron",
    ["Explain how farming began and its effects", "Describe village life and specialisation", "Explain the importance of iron working in Africa"],
    [("text", "Some 10 000 years ago, in several parts of the world, people learned to **plant crops** and **keep animals**. This change is called the **farming (agricultural) revolution**. It happened in different places at different times. In Africa, people domesticated crops such as sorghum, millet and yams and kept cattle, goats and sheep."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Effects of farming", "People could **settle** in one place and build **villages**; food became more plentiful and could be **stored**; the population grew; and some people could do other jobs (pottery, weaving, toolmaking, trading) instead of farming: this is **specialisation**. Settlements grew, leaders appeared, and trade between communities developed."),
     ("text", "Farming also brought problems: harvests could fail because of drought or pests, settlements could be attacked, and sharing land and water could cause disputes. People developed rules, customs and leaders to settle these problems, and the first **towns** and **states** later grew from such village communities."),
     ("key", "retenir", "The Iron Age", "People learned to extract **iron** from ore by heating it in a furnace, then shaped it with **blacksmith** skills. Iron hoes, axes, spears and knives were stronger than stone tools, so people could clear forest, farm more land and hunt and fight better. Iron working spread widely across Africa more than two thousand years ago, and blacksmiths often held a special place in society."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- The change to farming was gradual, not a single day; it happened at different times in different places.\n- Farming did not mean everybody became wealthier: some groups preferred hunting or herding.\n- Iron did not replace stone everywhere at the same time.")],
    [("Example 1: effects of farming", "Why did farming lead to the growth of villages?", ["Crops needed tending for months.", "Farmers had to stay near their fields and store the harvest.", "So they built permanent homes and villages."], "Farmers had to stay near fields and stores, so villages grew."),
     ("Example 2: advantages of iron", "Why was an iron hoe better than a stone hoe?", ["Iron is harder and less likely to break.", "It can be sharpened and shaped.", "So work was faster and more land could be cleared."], "It is stronger, can be sharpened and clears more land.")],
    F(cols("Before and after farming", [("Hunting-gathering", ["Move often", "Small groups", "Wild food"]), ("Farming", ["Settle", "Villages", "Stored food"])], w=380), "A change in way of life", "Two columns comparing hunting and gathering with farming."),
    [MCQ("Making a living by growing crops and keeping animals began in the", "New Stone Age", ["Old Stone Age", "20th century", "Middle Ages"], "Farming began in the Neolithic."),
     TF("The skill of the blacksmith was important in iron-working societies.", True, "Blacksmiths made tools and weapons."),
     MATCH("Match each change with its result.", [("Stored food", "Population grows"), ("Settlement", "Villages appear"), ("Iron tools", "More land cleared")], "Cause and effect in early societies."),
     MCQ("Specialisation means", "people doing different jobs instead of all farming", ["everyone doing the same job", "no trade", "no leaders"], "Pottery and weaving are examples.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Describe three effects of the use of iron on the lives of African societies.", "Iron tools allowed forests to be cleared and farming expanded, which fed more people; iron weapons improved hunting and defence; blacksmiths became valued specialists and trade in iron goods developed.", "1 mark per effect.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A village of early farmers keeps goats and grows millet.",
          [("mcq", "Keeping goats and growing millet shows", "farming", ["hunting-gathering only", "nomadic raiding", "city life"], "These are agricultural activities.", {}),
           ("tf", "Food storage helps people to survive bad seasons.", True, "Reserves protect against shortages.", {}),
           ("mcq", "A new job made possible by a food surplus is", "potter", ["hunter-gatherer", "cave painter only", "wild-fruit collector only"], "Surplus supports specialists.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two problems farming communities faced.", "Drought, pests and disease, attacks from others, and disputes over land and water.", "1 mark per problem.", {})])],
    [("The change from hunting to farming is called the", "farming revolution", ["industrial revolution", "iron revolution", "printing revolution"], "It changed ways of life."),
     ("Iron tools were better than stone tools because they were", "stronger and sharper", ["softer", "made of wood", "heavier than boats"], "Iron is stronger."),
     ("A food surplus allows", "specialisation and trade", ["no trade", "less farming", "no villages"], "Extra food supports other jobs."),
     ("A blacksmith is a", "person who works iron", ["farmer", "teacher", "fisherman"], "Smiths made tools."),
     ("A permanent home made possible by farming is a", "village", ["cave only", "ship", "tent only"], "Farmers settle.")],
    ["Teacher to verify the approximate date of the first farmers and the spread of ironworking in Africa (kept as 'about 10 000 years' and 'more than two thousand years ago')."], tag="Form 1 farming and iron")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH3 Egypt
c = p.chapter("egypt", "Ancient Egypt")
L = LS(c, "egypt-nile", "Ancient Egypt: the gift of the Nile",
    ["Explain why the Nile was important to Egypt", "Describe the pharaoh, society and religion", "Describe the achievements of the ancient Egyptians", "Explain how we know about ancient Egypt"],
    [("text", "Ancient Egypt grew along the **River Nile** in north-east Africa, in a land that is otherwise desert. Each year the river **flooded**, leaving rich black silt on its banks. Farmers grew wheat, barley and flax. The Greek writer Herodotus called Egypt the **'gift of the Nile'**. The river was also a highway for boats and carried stone for building."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Government and society", "The ruler of Egypt was the **pharaoh**, seen as a god-king, who owned the land and led the army and religion. Below him were **nobles and priests**, **officials and scribes** (who wrote records), **soldiers**, **craftworkers and merchants**, and a large number of **farmers** and workers. Egyptians worshipped many gods and believed in life after death."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Achievements", "- **Writing**: hieroglyphs on stone and, on **papyrus** (a paper made from a reed).\n- **Building**: the **pyramids**, tombs of pharaohs, built about 4500 years ago, and great temples.\n- **Science**: a calendar of 365 days, mathematics for measuring land, medicine, and **mummification**.\n- **Art**: painting, sculpture, jewellery."),
     ("text", "**How do we know?** Archaeologists have found tombs, paintings, statues and papyrus documents. The **Rosetta Stone**, found in 1799, had the same text in three scripts, which helped scholars to read hieroglyphs in the 1820s. Ancient Egypt influenced Greece and Rome, and also had links with other parts of Africa such as Nubia (Kush) to the south."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Egypt is part of Africa, and the Egyptians were African people with links to other Africans.\n- The pyramids were tombs, not just monuments.\n- Egyptian civilisation lasted thousands of years; do not treat it as one short period.")],
    [("Example 1: the Nile", "Explain why most Egyptians lived near the Nile.", ["Most of Egypt is desert with very little rain.", "The Nile gave water and the flood left fertile silt.", "So people lived along the river."], "Because the river gave water and fertile land in a desert."),
     ("Example 2: writing", "Why was papyrus useful to the Egyptians?", ["It was light and could be rolled and carried.", "Scribes could keep records on it.", "So government and trade could be recorded."], "It was a light writing material for records.")],
    F(shapes([T(210, 18, "The Nile and Egypt (schematic)", size=13, bold=True), RECT(20, 30, 380, 18, fill="lightblue", stroke="blue"), T(210, 43, "Mediterranean Sea (north)", size=11), LINE(210, 50, 210, 170, color="blue", width=6),
              RECT(130, 70, 60, 20, fill="lightgreen"), T(160, 85, "Fertile", size=11), RECT(230, 70, 60, 20, fill="lightgreen"), T(260, 85, "Fertile", size=11), T(75, 115, "Desert", size=12), T(350, 115, "Desert", size=12), T(210, 190, "Flows north to the sea", size=12)], 420, 200),
         "Egypt and the Nile", "A schematic map showing the Nile flowing north through desert to the sea with fertile land along its banks."),
    [MCQ("In Form 1 history, ancient Egypt was called the 'gift of the Nile' because", "the river's flood made the land fertile", ["it was rich in gold only", "it had many mountains", "it had no desert"], "The silt fed farming."),
     TF("The pharaoh was both ruler and religious leader.", True, "He was seen as a god-king."),
     MATCH("Match each Egyptian term with its meaning.", [("Scribe", "Official who wrote records"), ("Papyrus", "Paper made from reeds"), ("Pyramid", "Tomb for a pharaoh")], "Basic vocabulary."),
     MCQ("The Rosetta Stone was important because it", "helped scholars to read hieroglyphs", ["was a pyramid", "was a river", "was a mummy"], "It carried the same text in three scripts.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Describe four achievements of the ancient Egyptians.", "Hieroglyphic writing and papyrus; pyramids and temples; a 365-day calendar and mathematics; medicine and mummification; art and jewellery.", "1 mark per achievement.", points=4, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("Study a description of Egyptian life.",
          [("mcq", "Who kept the written records?", "scribes", ["farmers", "soldiers", "slaves only"], "Scribes were trained to write.", {}),
           ("tf", "Egypt gets plenty of rain, so the Nile was not needed.", False, "Egypt is a desert and depends on the Nile.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The pyramids were mainly", "tombs", ["markets", "schools", "ports"], "They were built for pharaohs.", {}),
           ("open", "Explain how the Nile helped trade and travel.", "Boats on the river carried people, goods and building stone north and south, linking towns along the Nile.", "1 mark for river transport; 1 mark for trade.", {})])],
    [("The river that gave life to ancient Egypt was the", "Nile", ["Niger", "Congo", "Wouri"], "The Nile flows north."),
     ("Egyptian writing is called", "hieroglyphs", ["alphabet of Greece", "cuneiform", "braille"], "Picture-like signs."),
     ("The ruler of ancient Egypt was the", "pharaoh", ["emperor of Rome", "sultan", "prime minister"], "Pharaoh is the title."),
     ("Mummification was linked to the belief in", "life after death", ["no gods", "farming only", "the sea"], "They preserved the body."),
     ("The Rosetta Stone was found in", "1799", ["1884", "1914", "1960"], "It was found by French soldiers in 1799.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: the approximate age of the pyramids (about 4500 years), the Rosetta Stone discovery (1799) and decipherment (1820s), and the wording about Herodotus."], tag="Form 1 Egypt")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH4 West African empires
c = p.chapter("westafrica", "Empires of West Africa")
L = LS(c, "trans-saharan-empires", "Trade across the Sahara and the empires of Ghana, Mali and Songhai",
    ["Describe the trans-Saharan trade", "Describe the rise of Ghana, Mali and Songhai", "Explain the importance of Mansa Musa and Timbuktu", "Explain why these empires declined"],
    [("text", "Camel caravans crossed the **Sahara** carrying goods between West Africa and North Africa. The Sahara trade carried **salt** (from desert mines) and cloth, copper and beads south, and **gold**, ivory, kola nuts and slaves north. Traders and rulers grew wealthy. Towns on the edge of the desert, such as Timbuktu and Gao, became great markets. The trade also spread Islam and the Arabic script."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Ghana, Mali and Songhai", "- **Ghana** (ancient Ghana, called Wagadu): grew rich from taxing the gold and salt trade, and was strong in the early centuries of the second millennium.\n- **Mali**: rose in the 13th century; its great ruler **Mansa Musa** travelled on pilgrimage to Mecca in **1324**, with so much gold that people spoke of it for years.\n- **Songhai**: strong in the 15th–16th centuries; its capital was **Gao**."),
     ("text", "**Timbuktu** was a centre of trade and learning, with mosques, schools and libraries where scholars copied and studied books. Rulers used governors, soldiers, taxes and a system of provinces to hold the empires together. Farmers, herders, fishermen, blacksmiths and craftworkers supported them; Songhai was strengthened by a strong army and river canoes on the Niger."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Decline", "Empires weakened because of **quarrels over succession**, **revolts** by conquered peoples, difficult distances to control, and **attacks** from outside. Songhai was defeated in **1591** by a Moroccan army with firearms at Tondibi. Later, new trade routes by sea to the coast of West Africa reduced the importance of the Sahara routes."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Ancient Ghana is not the same place as modern Ghana; the modern country took the name in 1957 to honour the older empire.\n- The dates of these empires are approximate and historians differ.\n- Wealth came from controlling trade and taxing it, not only from owning gold mines.")],
    [("Example 1: caravan trade", "Name two goods carried south and two carried north on the Sahara routes.", ["Salt, cloth, copper and beads came from the north.", "Gold, ivory, kola nuts and slaves went north.", "So salt and cloth south; gold and ivory north."], "South: salt, cloth. North: gold, ivory."),
     ("Example 2: why the empires were powerful", "Explain how a ruler of Ghana became rich without owning every gold mine.", ["Traders had to pass through his lands.", "He taxed the goods entering and leaving.", "So he earned from the trade."], "By taxing the trade that passed through his empire.")],
    F(shapes([T(210, 18, "Trans-Saharan trade (schematic)", size=13, bold=True), RECT(20, 30, 380, 18, fill="lightblue", stroke="blue"), T(210, 43, "North Africa", size=11),
              RECT(20, 55, 380, 100, fill="lightyellow"), T(210, 110, "Sahara desert", size=12), LINE(300, 150, 300, 60, arrow="end", color="red", width=3), T(335, 90, "Gold, ivory", size=11), LINE(120, 60, 120, 150, arrow="end", color="blue", width=3), T(70, 100, "Salt, cloth", size=11),
              RECT(20, 160, 380, 30, fill="lightgreen"), T(210, 180, "West Africa (Ghana, Mali, Songhai; Timbuktu, Gao)", size=11)], 420, 200),
         "Caravan routes across the Sahara", "A schematic with the Sahara between North Africa and West Africa and arrows for goods going north and south."),
    [MCQ("Which of these was a main item carried north across the Sahara?", "gold", ["tractors", "machines", "oil"], "Gold and ivory were main exports."),
     TF("Mansa Musa made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324.", True, "It made Mali famous across the Mediterranean world."),
     MATCH("Match each empire to a feature.", [("Ghana", "Rich from taxing gold and salt trade"), ("Mali", "Ruler Mansa Musa"), ("Songhai", "Capital at Gao, defeated in 1591")], "Key features of the empires."),
     MCQ("Timbuktu was important as a", "centre of trade and learning", ["seaport only", "volcano", "refugee camp only"], "It had markets and scholars.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain three reasons why the West African empires declined.", "Quarrels over who should rule, revolts by conquered peoples, the difficulty of governing large distances, attacks from outside such as the Moroccan invasion of Songhai, and the shift of trade to sea routes.", "1 mark per reason with explanation.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A merchant joins a caravan from Timbuktu to North Africa.",
          [("mcq", "The animal that carried the goods was the", "camel", ["elephant", "horse only", "donkey only"], "Camels cross deserts well.", {}),
           ("tf", "Salt was valuable in West Africa.", True, "It was needed by people and animals.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Which religion spread along the trade routes?", "Islam", ["no religion at all", "only ancient Egyptian religion", "Roman gods"], "Traders brought Islam.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two benefits that trade brought to the empires.", "Wealth from taxes, growth of towns and markets, new ideas and writing, and contacts with other peoples.", "1 mark per benefit.", {})])],
    [("The empire famed for its gold trade in ancient West Africa was", "Ghana", ["Egypt", "Rome", "Kongo only"], "Ancient Ghana was rich in gold trade."),
     ("Mansa Musa was ruler of", "Mali", ["Songhai", "Egypt", "Kush"], "He made the pilgrimage in 1324."),
     ("The capital of the Songhai empire was", "Gao", ["Foumban", "Douala", "Cairo"], "Gao was on the Niger."),
     ("Songhai was defeated in 1591 by", "a Moroccan army", ["a French army", "a German army", "a British army"], "Moroccan forces with guns won at Tondibi."),
     ("The Sahara caravans mostly used", "camels", ["trains", "lorries", "ships"], "Camels carried goods across the desert.")],
    ["Teacher to verify dates and rulers: Mansa Musa's pilgrimage (1324), the Songhai defeat (1591), approximate periods of Ghana (early second millennium), Mali (13th–15th century) and Songhai (15th–16th century), and the origin of the modern name Ghana."], tag="Form 1 West African empires")

# ------------------------------------------------------------ CH5 East & Southern Africa
c = p.chapter("eastsouth", "East and Southern Africa")
L = LS(c, "swahili-zimbabwe", "The Swahili coast and Great Zimbabwe",
    ["Describe the Swahili city-states and their trade", "Describe Great Zimbabwe and its importance", "Compare the trade of East and West Africa"],
    [("text", "Along the **East African coast**, from Somalia to Mozambique, lived the **Swahili** people in trading **city-states** such as **Mombasa**, **Kilwa** and Zanzibar. Their language, **Kiswahili**, is an African (Bantu) language with many borrowed Arabic words. They traded across the **Indian Ocean** with Arabia, Persia, India and China, using sailing boats (dhows) and the **monsoon winds**, which blow one way for part of the year and the other way for the rest."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Swahili trade", "Swahili ports **exported** gold, ivory, timber, iron and slaves and **imported** cloth, beads, glass, porcelain and spices. The merchants built houses, mosques and palaces of coral stone. Most Swahili towns were ruled by their own sultans or elders and did not form a single empire."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Great Zimbabwe", "**Great Zimbabwe** (in the present country of Zimbabwe) was a large stone-walled town of the Shona-speaking peoples, flourishing around the 11th–15th centuries. Its stone walls and towers were built without mortar. It was rich in **cattle**, **gold** and ivory, which were traded with the coast and then across the ocean. Its name means 'stone houses'."),
     ("text", "The towns of the coast and the interior were linked by trade: gold from the Zimbabwe plateau reached Kilwa and other ports, and was sent to Arabia and beyond. Objects such as Chinese porcelain and glass beads have been found at Great Zimbabwe, which shows these long-distance links. The **Kingdom of Aksum** (in present-day Ethiopia) earlier traded with Egypt, Arabia and India and became a Christian kingdom in the 4th century."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- The Swahili coast was not one country or empire; it was many independent city-states.\n- Great Zimbabwe was built by Africans, as archaeological evidence shows.\n- Monsoon winds are seasonal winds; they are not the same as the harmattan of West Africa.")],
    [("Example 1: monsoon trade", "Why were monsoon winds important to the Swahili traders?", ["The winds blow towards India for part of the year.", "They blow back to Africa later in the year.", "Sailors could plan return voyages with the winds."], "They allowed regular voyages across the Indian Ocean."),
     ("Example 2: Great Zimbabwe", "Give two reasons why Great Zimbabwe became important.", ["It controlled valuable cattle and gold.", "It was connected to the coast by trade.", "So it became rich and powerful."], "Cattle, gold and trade links.")],
    F(flow("Indian Ocean trade", ["Zimbabwe|gold", "Swahili|ports", "Arabia and|India", "Cloth, beads|come back"], per_row=4), "East African trade links", "Four boxes showing gold from Zimbabwe going through Swahili ports to Arabia and India and goods returning."),
    [MCQ("Swahili traders crossed the Indian Ocean using", "monsoon winds", ["trains", "motorboats", "steam engines"], "The seasonal winds helped sailing."),
     TF("Great Zimbabwe was built by Africans.", True, "Archaeology shows it was built by Shona-speaking peoples."),
     MATCH("Match each place with its description.", [("Kilwa", "Swahili trading city-state"), ("Great Zimbabwe", "Stone-walled capital in the interior"), ("Aksum", "Kingdom in present-day Ethiopia")], "These are three places of early East and Southern Africa."),
     MCQ("Which two goods did the Swahili ports mainly import?", "cloth and beads", ["cars and radios", "computers and phones", "oil and cement"], "Goods from Asia and Arabia.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain how the trade of the East African coast was different from the Trans-Saharan trade.", "Swahili trade went by sea across the Indian Ocean on dhows using monsoon winds, linking East Africa with Arabia, India and China; the Sahara trade went overland by camel between West and North Africa.", "1 mark: sea vs land; 1 mark: partners; 1 mark: transport.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("An archaeologist finds a Chinese bowl and local gold beads at Great Zimbabwe.",
          [("mcq", "This suggests that Great Zimbabwe", "had trade links with distant lands", ["was isolated", "had no gold", "had no people"], "The bowl came from far away.", {}),
           ("tf", "Kiswahili is a Bantu language with many Arabic words.", True, "It reflects long contacts.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The Swahili city-states were ruled by", "their own leaders (sultans or elders)", ["one emperor only", "foreign armies only", "no one"], "They were independent.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two reasons why gold was important for trade.", "It was valuable and easy to carry, was wanted in other lands, and could pay for imports such as cloth and beads.", "1 mark per reason.", {})])],
    [("A dhow is a", "sailing boat of the Indian Ocean", ["train", "plane", "truck"], "Dhows were used by Swahili traders."),
     ("Great Zimbabwe means", "stone houses", ["golden river", "big sea", "iron fort"], "It refers to stone buildings."),
     ("Which of these is a Swahili city-state?", "Mombasa", ["Gao", "Timbuktu", "Foumban"], "Mombasa is on the Kenyan coast."),
     ("The Kingdom of Aksum was in the area of present-day", "Ethiopia", ["Ghana", "Cameroon", "Gabon"], "Aksum was in the Ethiopian highlands."),
     ("Monsoon winds are", "seasonal winds that change direction", ["winds that never blow", "cold ice winds", "winds of the desert only"], "They help ocean voyages.")],
    ["Teacher to verify approximate dates of Great Zimbabwe (11th–15th centuries) and Aksum's adoption of Christianity (4th century), the list of coastal city-states and the goods traded."], tag="Form 1 East and Southern Africa")

